?$LOG
 $LOG logfile
 $NOLOG
 
   The $LOG command causes a  file "logfile.log" to be created and all
   command input  to be echoed  on that file.   Unfortunately, printed
   output  cannot be  duplicated  on that  file.   The $NOLOG  command
   closes any log file.

?-
?COMMANDS

   In the MUVI  system commands are keywords followed  by zero, one or
   more parameters.   No distinction is  made between upper  and lower
   case, and you can type numbers in free format.

?-
?FONT
 FONT fontstyle fontsize

   This command  attempts to select the  style and size of  font to be
   used; however, it is limited by those supported by the device.  The
   "fontstyle" parameter  defaults to "Screen", and  can take optional
   values "Courier" and "Times";  others will be supported later.  The
   "fontsize" parameter  is measured in screen pixels  (e.g. 1/72 inch
   on PostScript, 0.01 inch on SGI).

?-
?GRIDMODEL

   A  Grid model consists  one or  more time  frames, with  each frame
   being  a 1-,2-  or  3-dimensional array  containing solution  data.
   These  models are  generally created  from an  application  such as
   COBRA, but can also be written to and read from files

?-
?MODELS

   Models  can either represent  Fluid or  Solid datasets.   The Fluid
   datasets are divided into two categories: Grid and Scatter.

?-
?SCATTERMODEL

   A Scatter model  consists one or more time  frames, with each frame
   consisting a list of solution  points defined in 1-,2- or 3-dimens-
   -ional space.

?-
?SOLIDMODEL

   A  Solid model  consists one  or  more groups  of solid  primitives
   (spheres, cylinders, blocks, etc.), defined in 3-dimensional space.
   Each  group is  identified  by a  "tag"  which can  be mapped  onto
   material properties at the time of display.

?-
?XWINDOWS

   The OPEN  command supports a  range of XWindows device  types.  The
   general form of the device name is X_cmap_cmethod.  where "cmap" is
   one of MY, CLONE or DEF, and "cmethod" is one of RGB,HSV,HV,CMAP.

   "cmap"  determines how  MUVI  uses the  colourmap  resource of  the
   display.  MY means that MUVI attempts to create a new colourmap for
   its exclusive  use.  On displays with a  single hardware colourmap,
   this will cause  "flashing" as the mouse pointer  is moved from one
   window  to another.   CLONE means  that MUVI  takes a  copy  of the
   prevailing default  colourmap, and  uses the remaining  free colour
   slots for its  own purpose.  This should reduce  the probability of
   "flashing".

   DEF  means that  the actual  default colourmap  is used,  with MUVI
   adding in its own colours.  This will normally work on any display,
   but  is unfriendly  to other  programs  which may  use the  default
   colourmap.

   "cmethod"  determines how MUVI  arranges its  colours, and  has the
   effect  of enhancing  (say)  solid shading  or  colour contours  on
   displays with a restricted number  of colours.  RGB means that MUVI
   arranges the  colours in an RGB  cube; this is  the most democratic
   approach, but  may not produce  very good shaded surfaces.   HSV is
   better for  pure and pastel surface  shading.  HV is  good for pure
   shaded surfaces and colour  contours.  CMAP allows MUVI to allocate
   any  number of  shading and  colour  values for  a given  activity,
   subject to those available.

   The best way to choose a suitable device is to try them and see.

   In order  for an  X window to  be created, the  DISPLAY environment
   variable must be defined with the form

      setenv DISPLAY machine:0

   where "machine" is the hostname of the display machine.
?-
?XFONTS

   There is  no standard for  the text fonts  that are available  on a
   given display.  However, there is a mechanism for mapping the names
   and sizes of  fonts in the FONT command  to actual fonts associated
   with the display.   This mapping is done by  a text file containing
   lines of the form:

      font_style font_size font_name

   where "font_style"  and "font_size" are the parameters  to the FONT
   command, and are chosen by the user, and "font_name" is the name of
   a font as indicated by the "xlsfonts" command.  For example:

      Roman 12 variable
      Courier 12 fixed

   The  name of  this file  is either  defined by  the MUVI_FONT_TABLE
   environment variable,  or else in the local  file "muvi.fonts".  If
   missing, a default font will always be available.

   Note that the fonts are  available from the Display you are sitting
   in  front of,  rather than  the computer  on which  the  program is
   running ?- ?POSTSCRIPT The OPEN  command supports the PS and PS_RGB
   device types.  The PS device  is for monochrome printers; PS_RGB is
   for colour printers or for grey-scales.

   The OPEN  command creates a file with  name <worksurface>.ps, which
   must be prefixed by a "plot.ps"  header file before being sent to a
   printer.   Some modification  of the  ".ps" file  may be  needed to
   insert the plot into a document.

?-
?ARROW
 ARROW

   This command  specifies that vector  fields are to be  displayed as
   arrow maps.  It negates the effect of the STREAM command.

?-
?BAND
 BAND
 NOBAND

   The BAND command specifies  that 2-dimensional scalar fields are to
   be displayed as colour range maps; NOBAND avoids these.  Bands with
   indicies 0, 1  and 2 are allows, with scalar  ranges defined by the
   BANDR command,  and revoked by  the NOBANDR command.   BAND removes
   the effect of COLOUR and CONTOUR.

?-
?BANDR
 BANDR band_index qmin qmax
 NOBANDR

   The BANDR command  specifies the scalar range "qmin"  to "qmax" for
   the band with index "band_index"; the NOBANDR removes this.

?-
?BINARY_DATA
 BINARY_DATA

   This command toggles between text  and binary mode for writing MUVI
   (etc.) files.  The default is binary.

?-
?BOX
 BOX
 NOBOX

   The BOX  command specifies that graph  axes are to  be designed and
   draw  for each  plotting  instruction (P,  V,  etc.); NOBOX  allows
   subsequent  plotting instructions  to cause  overlaid  graphs where
   this is sensible.

?-
?CA
 CA

   This command is only useful on terminals such as the Pericom MG and
   MX  series which support  separate alpha  and graphic  screens.  It
   prints  24 blank  lines and  then waits  for the  user to  type the
   RETURN key.   It effectively clears the scrolling  alpha screen and
   lets you see the overlay graphics.  ?- ?CAPTION CAPTION CaptionText
   NOCAPTION

   This command  specifies a new  caption line for any  future graphs.
   The number of  caption lines that can be  displayed is specified by
   the  CAPTION_LINES command.  You  can reset  the caption  and start
   again with NOCAPTION.

?-
?CAPTION_ABOVE
 CAPTION_ABOVE

   This command specifies that captions are to be drawn above the next
   graph.  It negates the effect of CAPTION_BELOW.

a
?-
?CAPTION_BELOW
 CAPTION_BELOW

   This command specifies that captions are to be drawn below the next
   graph.  It negates the effect of CAPTION_ABOVE.

?-
?CAPTION_INSET
 CAPTION_INSET CaptionInset

   This command specifies the size  of the left margin for the caption
   measured in character widths.

?-
?CAPTION_LINES
 CAPTION_LINES CaptionLines

   This command  specifies the  number of caption  lines which  can be
   stored with the  CAPTION command and displayed on  graphs.  A value
   of zero means that no space is reserved for captions on the graph.

?-
?CLEAR
 CLEAR

   This  command   erases  the  current   worksurface,  including  all
   subwindows contained on it.

?-
?CLOSE
 CLOSE Worksurface

   This command closes the worksurface identified by "WorkSurface"

?-
?CLOSEW
 CLOSEW subwin

   This  command closes  (deletes) a  subwindow.  If  it  is currently
   selected, then the base window ("whole") is selected in its place.

?-
?COLOUR
 COLOUR
 NOCOLOUR

   The COLOUR  command specifies that 2-dimensional  scalar fields are
   to be displayed as colour  maps; NOCOLOUR avoids these.  The colour
   gradation is controlled by the NCOLOUR command.

?-
?CONT
 CONT ContourLevel

   This  command specifies  a single  contour level.   It  removes the
   effect of CONTS and NCONT commands.

?-
?CONTS
 CONTS ncont ContourLevels[1..ncont]

   This  command  specifies  "ncont"  individual contour  levels.   It
   removes the effect of the CONT and NCONT commands.

?-
?CONTOUR
 CONTOUR
 NOCONTOUR

   The CONTOUR command specifies  that 2-dimensional scalar fields are
   to be displayed as contour maps; NOCONTOUR avoids this.

?-
?CONTOUR_LABEL
 CONTOUR_LABEL
 NOCONTOUR_LABEL

   These  commands toggle  whether  contours are  labelled with  their
   indicies.

?-
?DATA_COORD
 DATA_COORD position
NODATA_COORD

   This  commands specifies  the coordinate  position for  the cutting
   plane for a DATA_ORTHO scalar plot in 3D.

?-
?DATA_FRAME
 DATA_FRAME
 NODATA_FRAME

   These  commands determine  whether  a  space frame  is  drawn on  a
   3-dimensional picture.

?-
?DATA_SHADE
 DATA_SHADE

   This command selects data shading  of solid surfaces as effected in
   3-dimensional versions of the P command.  It negates the DATA_ORTHO
   command.

?-
?DATA_ORTHO
 DATA_ORTHO id

   This  command   selects  data  planes   to  be  colour   shaded  in
   3-dimensional versions of the P command.  It negates the DATA_SHADE
   command.

?-
?ER
 ER

   This  command erases  the  current subwindow,  including any  graph
   contained in it.

?-
?EXPORT
 EXPORT filename

   After this command  has been issued, the graphics  file is exported
   to filename.xq  as a text file  when the P command  is issued. This
   file can then be plotted with the READXQ command.  The graph is not
   plotted. Subsequent P commands plot as usual. Note that it will not
   overwrite an existing file.

?-
?G
 G

   This command causes  the grid associated with the  current model to
   be displayed.

?-
?GREY_RAIN
 GREY_RAIN
 NOGREY_RAIN

   This  command specifies that  colour maps  use s  linear grey-scale
   rather than  a "rainbow".  This is helpful  when producing pictures
   for monochrome printers.

?-
?HBORDER
 HBORDER bord

   This  command  specifies  a  fractional border  in  the  horizontal
   direction for a graph within a subwindow.

?-
?HELP
 HELP topic

   This command obtains help text  on the chosen "topic".  If you want
   to find out about available topics, type: HELP TOPICS

?-
?HSURF
 HSURF height

   This command selects the relative  height of the vertical extent in
   a scalar surface plot: q[x,y].  Default is 0.5 .

?-
?KEY
 KEY
 NOKEY

   These  commands toggle  whether a  key is  drawn  for 1-dimensional
   graphs.

?-
?KEY_LABEL
 KEY_LABEL label
 NOKEY_LABEL

   These commands select or revoke the label to be associated with the
   next key entry for a 1-dimensional graph.

?-
?KEY_LABEL_FIELD
 KEY_LABEL_FIELD field

   This specifies  the maximum number of  characters for a  label in a
   Key.

?-
?KEY_NUMBER_FIELD
 KEY_NUMBER_FIELD field

   This specifies the  maximum number of characters in  a number field
   in a Key.

?-
?KEY_SIZE
 KEY_SIZE size

   This command specifies the number of entries (lines) in a Key.

?-
?LABEL_FIELD
 LABEL_FIELD field

   This command  specifies the maximum  field width for  graph labels.
   It is used in axis placement.

?-
?LIGHT_ALTD
 LIGHT_ALTD altd

   This  command  specifies the  altitudinal  angle  (degrees) of  the
   light-point seen from the view-focus.

?-
?LIGHT_AZIM
 LIGHT_AZIM azim

   This  command  specifies  the  azimuthal  angle  (degrees)  of  the
   light-point seen from the view-focus.

?-
?LIGHT_DISTANCE
 LIGHT_DISTANCE distance
 NOLIGHT_DISTANCE

   The   LIGHT_DISTANCE  command   specifies  the   distance   of  the
   light-point  from  the  view-focus;  the  NOLIGHT_DISTANCE  command
   removes this.

?-
?LIGHT_FUSION
 LIGHT_FUSION fusion

   This command specifies what fraction of the light intensity (at the
   view-focus) is diffuse; the remainder  is expected to come from the
   light-point.

?-
?LINE
 LINE LineIndex
 NOLINE

   The LINE command specifies the  line style index for scalar graphs.
   By default the  line style index is 1,  which corresponds to solid;
   other line  styles are device  specific.  The NOLINE  command means
   that no connecting lines are drawn between points on a graph.

?-
?LINE_PALET
 LINE_PALET PaletIndex

   This  selects a  colour PaletIndex  (in the  range 1  to  7), which
   determines the  colour of lines  and markers drawn  (initially only
   1-dimensional  scalar graphs).   The current  RGB settings  for the
   palet are  printed by the VSHOW  command, and you  can modify these
   with the PALET command.

?-
?LOG
 LOG
 NOLOG

   These commands toggle whether  scalars are converted to LOG10 prior
   to plotting.

?-
?MARKER
 MARKER MarkerIndex
 NOMARKER

   The MARKER  command specifies the  marker index for  scalar graphs.
   Indices in the  range 1 to 4 are  supported, independent of device.
   The NOMARKER command avoids drawing markers at data points.

?-
?MARK_SCALE
 MARK_SCALE MarkerScale

   This command  specifies the marker  size in terms of  the character
   height.

?-
?MASK
 MASK Coord Position

   This  command specifies  that the  number of  dimensions of  a Grid
   model is to be reduced for display by fixing the "Coord" coordinate
   at "Position" in that direction.   Its effect can be removed by the
   UNMASK command.

?-
?MASKI
 MASKI Coord ArrayOffset

   This command specifies that the  the number of dimensions of a Grid
   model is to be reduced for display by fixing the "Coord" coordinate
   direction at array offset "ArrayOffset".  Its effect can be removed
   by the UNMASK command.

?-
?MATERIAL
 MATERIAL material RGB red green blue END
 NOMATERIALS

   The MATERIAL  command introduces the  diffuse reflective properties
   of  a named  material.  The  elaborate  syntax is  to allow  future
   additions.  NOMATERIALS removes all  materials except the one named
   "DEFAULT".

?-
?MATERIAL_colour
 MATERIAL_RED material red
 MATERIAL_GREEN material green
 MATERIAL_BLUE material blue

   These commands specify the reflection coefficient for a Material in
   Red,  Green  or  Blue.  If  the  Material  is  new, it  is  created
   dynamically and assigned default properties.

?-
?NCOLOUR
 NCOLOUR ncolour

   This command specifies  the number of colour gradations  to be used
   in scalar colour maps.

?-
?NCONT
 NCONT ncont

   This command  specifies the number  of contour levels which  are to
   cover the scalar range; it removes the effect of CONT and CONTS.

   See also:  CONT, CONTS.

?-
?NSHADE
 NSHADE nshade

   This  command selects  the  number of  colour  gradations used  for
   light-shading.   Note that  in  light-data shading  models on  8bit
   devices, the product NCOLOUR*NSHADE may be limited to about 100.

?-
?NUMBER_FIELD
 NUMBER_FIELD field

   This  command  specifies the  maximum  number  of  characters in  a
   numeric field; used to design axis placement.

?-
?OPEN
 OPEN WorkSurface DeviceType

   This command opens a graphics worksurface of type "DeviceType" with
   user-supplied name "WorkSurface".  The "DeviceType" is from:

     PS*     Postscript (various)
     X*      XWindows (various)

   The newly opened worksurface is selected as active.

?-
?OPENW
 OPENW subwin hmin hmax vmin vmax

   This  command  opens  a  subwindow  on the  current  graphics  work
   surface, with name "subwin" and fractional extents "hmin" to "hmax"
   and  "vmin" to  "vmax" in  the horizontal  and  vertical directions
   respectively.   A  subwindow is  automatically  selected after  its
   creation.

   When a worksurface is opened,  a "base" subwindow called "whole" is
   created, filling the entire worksurface.

?-
?P
 P

   This command  causes the scalar  field associated with  the current
   fluid model  to be  displayed.  The field  value are  calculated as
   specified by the PLOT command.  The method of display is controlled
   by various options such as COLOUR, CONTOUR, BAND.

?-
?PALET
 PALET PaletIndex red green blue

   This command  allows you to change  the mix of red,  green and blue
   (all in range  0.0 to 1.0), for a given entry  in the colour palet.
   The PaletIndex is in the range 1 to 7.  The current settings can be
   seen with the VSHOW command.

?-
?PAPER
 PAPER Hinch Vinch

   This command specifies the horizontal and vertical size of the next
   worksurface opened  by OPEN,  as measured in  inches.  It  only has
   effect on workstations and plotters where this is supported.

?-
?POSITION
 POSITION Hinch Vinch

   This  command specifies  the  precise position  of the  bottom-left
   corner of  the next worksurface to  be opened.  It  applies only to
   workstations and plotters which support this.

?-
?Q
 Q
   This command terminates executaion of the program.
?-
?QLABEL
 QLABEL qlabel
 NOQLABEL

   The  QLABEL command  specifies  a label  for  the displayed  scalar
   quantity; NOQLABEL removes this.

?-
?QR
 QR qmin qmax
 NOQR

   The QR command  specifies the minimum and maximum  scalar values to
   be plotted.  The NOQR command removes the effect of this.

?-
?READXQ
 READXQ filename

   The READQ command reads a text file filename.xq containing data and
   plots it. For one dimensional data the file should be of the form

     1
     title
     x y
     x_1 y_1
     .
     .

   where title is the  title (no spaces), x, y are the  names of the x
   and y  coordinates, x_1, y_1  etc are the  x, y coordinates  of the
   data points.

   For two dimensions, the file should be of the form

     nd nl1 nl2 ..
     title
     x y var_name
     x_1    y_1 var_value
     x_2    y_1 var_value
     .
     x_nl1  y_1 var_value
     x_1    y_2 var_value
     .

   where nd  is the number of  dimensions, nl1, nl2 are  the number of
   points in the x, y directions (nl1xnl2 matrix), title is the title,
   x,  y are  the coordinate  names and  var_name is  the name  of the
   variable.   The coordinates of  the data  points must  be uniformly
   spaced with the x  coordinate varying most rapidly. The arrangement
   for three dimensions is similar. The EXPORT command produces a file
   of this type.

?-
?RPAPER
 RPAPER Hsize Vsize

   This command specifies the relative size of the next worksurface to
   be  opened  by  the  OPEN  command.  This  only  applies  to  work-
   -stations and plotters where this is supported.

?-
?RPOSITION
 RPOSITION Hrel Vrel

   This  command specifies  the relative  position of  the  next work-
   surface to be opened by  OPEN.  It only applies to workstations and
   plotters  where this  is supported.   Values  of 0.0  mean Left  or
   Bottom, whilst values of 1.0 mean Right or Top, as appropriate.

?-
?S
 S

   This command  causes the current  solid model to be  displayed.  At
   present a ray-trace method is employed.

?-
?SCALES
 SCALES
 NOSCALES

   These commands toggle whether a  box containing scales is drawn for
   colour, contour plots (etc.).

?-
?SCALP
 SCALP
 NOSCALP

   These commands toggle whether a 2-dimensional scalar field is drawn
   as a surface.  That is, the scalar is promoted to a coordinate.

?-
?SELECT
 SELECT WorkSurface

   This command selects the named "WorkSurface" to be active and hence
   receive future display output.

?-
?SELECTW
 SELECTW subwin

   This command selects a named subwindow for drawing.

?-
?SKY
 SKY red green blue

   This command specifies  the red, green and blue  intensities of the
   background sky (values in range 0.0 to 1.0).

?-
?STREAM
 STREAM n x y z dx dy dz

   The  STREAM  command  spceifies  that  velocity fields  are  to  be
   displayed by means of streamlines.

   The starting points for streamlines are chosen from the
   expression [x+i*dx,y+i*dy,z+i*dz], for (i=0,n-1), where
   [x,y,z] are the model coordinates.

?-
?TAG
 TAG tag material
 NOTAGS

   The TAG  command makes an  association between a solid  model "tag"
   and a "material"; NOTAGS removes all such associations.

?-
?TICK_SCALE
 TICK_SCALE scale

   This command specifies  the size of axis ticks  relative to current
   character height.   Negative values mean that the  ticks point into
   the graph.

?-
?TLABEL
 TLABEL tlabel
 NOTLABEL

   The  TLABEL  command  specifies  a  label for  the  displayed  time
   coordinate; NOTLABEL removes this.

?-
?TR
 TR tmin tmax
 NOTR

   The TR command specifies the  minimum and maximum time values to be
   displayed; NOTR removes this.

?-
?UNMASK
 UNMASK coord

   This command  removes a coordinate  masks specified by the  MASK or
   MASKI commands.   In this, "coord"  is either a coordinate  name or
   "*" (all coordinates).

?-
?V
 V

   This command  causes the vector  field associated with  the current
   model  to  be plotted.   The  values of  the  vector  field are  as
   specified by  the VPLOT  command.  The field  will be  displayed by
   Arrow or Streamline plots as requested.

?-
?VBORDER
 VBORDER bord

   This  command  specifies  a   fractional  border  in  the  vertical
   direction for a graph within a subwindow.

?-
?VIDEOC
 VIDEOC file

   This  command  captures  the  current worksurface  ("sgi*"  devices
   only),  in a  file  "file.img",  which can  be  transferred to  the
   U-matic video  machine, or pasted using the  IRIX "ipaste" command.
   The  SGI screen  is  nominally 100  pixels  per inch,  so that  the
   768x575 PAL size can theoretically matched by

     PAPER 7.68 5.75

   however, you should leave a border, perhaps by:

     OPENW INNER 0.1 0.9 0.1 0.9

?-
?VIEW_ALTD
 VIEW_ALTD altd

   This command  specifies the altitudinal  angle (in degrees)  of the
   view-point.

?-
?VIEW_AZIM
 VIEW_AZIM azim

   This  command specifies  the azimuthal  angle (in  degrees)  of the
   view-point.

?-
?VIEW_DISTANCE
 VIEW_DISTANCE distance
 NOVIEW_DISTANCE

   The VIEW_DISTANCE command specifies  the distance of the view-point
   from the view-focus.

?-
?VIEW_NEAR
 VIEW_NEAR distance
 NOVIEW_NEAR

   The VIEW_NEAR command specifies the distance of the view-point from
   the near clipping plane.

   This may not be supported.

?-
?VIEW_FAR
 VIEW_FAR distance
 NOVIEW_FAR

   The VIEW_FAR command specifies  the distance of the view-point from
   the far clipping plane.

   This may not be supported.
?-
?VIEW_FOCUS
 VIEW_FOCUS {x,y,z}
 NOVIEW_FOCUS

   The VIEW_FOCUS command  specifies the cartesian coordinates (x,y,z)
   of the view-focus.

?-
?VIEW_ANGLEV
 VIEW_ANGLEV anglev

   This  command specifies  the angle  (in degrees)  subtended  by the
   vertical axis of the view-surface.

?-
?VIEW_ASPECT
 VIEW_ASPECT aspect

   This command  specifies the width  of the view-surface in  terms of
   its height.

?-
?VMARK
 VMARK marker
 NOVMARK

   These commands  select or revoke a  marker to be used  as the pivot
   point for a velocity arrow or streamline.

?-
?VMAX
 VMAX vmax
 NOVMAX

   The VMAX  command specifies the  maximum velocity to  be displayed;
   the NOVMAX command removes this.

?-
?VPLOT
 VPLOT Coord Scalar

   This command  makes an association between  a coordinate direction,
   "Coord", and a "Scalar" value representing the fluid velocity along
   that  direction.    The  "Coord"  names  for   each  geometry  are:
   Cartesian(X,Y,Z), Cylindrical(R,Z,Phi), Spherical(R,Theta,Phi).

?-
?VPRINT
 VPRINT

   This  command prints  the list  of  open worksurfaces  and the  one
   crrently selected as active.

?-
?VRATE
 VRATE vrate

   This command specifies the rate  at which velocity arrows are to be
   taken from a the array of a  Grid model; a value 1 means take every
   array element, 2 means every other array element, and so on.

?-
?VRATIO
 VRATIO vratio

   This  command specifies  the velocity  arrow size  in terms  of the
   character size, and applies to Scatter models.

?-
?VSCALE
 VSCALE vscale

   This command specifies  a scaling to be applied  to velocity arrows
   in order to reduce clutter.

?-
?VSHOW
 VSHOW

   This  command prints details  of the  current worksurface/subwindow
   and prevailing plotting options.

?-
?WIRE_FRAME
 WIRE_FRAME
 NOWIRE_FRAME

   These commands  determine whether 3-dimensional  pictures including
   solids and surfaces are "enhanced" by wire-frame outlines.

?-
?XASPECT
 XASPECT aspect

   This command specifies the way  in which the first space coordinate
   is stretched relative to the others.

   See also:  YASPECT, ZASPECT.

?-
?XLABEL
 XLABEL xlabel
 NOXLABEL

   The  XLABEL   command  specifies  a  label  for   the  first  space
   coordinate; NOXLABEL removes this.

?-
?XR
 XR xmin xmax
 NOXR

   The XR command  specifies minimum and maximum values  for the first
   space coordinate  in subsequent graphs; NOXR removes  the effect of
   this.

?-
?YASPECT
 YASPECT aspect

   This command specifies the way in which the second space coordinate
   is stretched relative to the others.

?-
?ZASPECT
 ZASPECT aspect

   This command specifies the way  in which the third space coordinate
   is stretched relative to the others.

?-
?YLABEL
 YLABEL ylabel
 NOYLABEL

   The YLABEL command specifies a label for the second space
   coordinate; NOYLABEL removes this.

?-
?YR
 YR ymin ymax
 NOYR

   The YR command specifies minimum  and maximum values for the second
   space coordinate  in subsequent graphs; NOYR removes  the effect of
   this.

?-
?ZLABEL
 ZLABEL zlabel
 NOZLABEL

   The  ZLABEL   command  specifies  a  label  for   the  third  space
   coordinate; NOZLABEL removes this.

?-
?ZR
 ZR zmin zmax
 NOZR

   The ZR command  specifies minimum and maximum values  for the third
   space coordinate  in subsequent graphs; NOZR removes  the effect of
   this.

?-
